The genealogy of Jesus Christ.
This is the genealogy of the family of Jesus Christ. He is the son of David. He is also the son of Abraham.
Abraham was a father of Isaac. Isaac was a father of Jacob. Jacob was a father of Judah and his brothers.
Judah was the father of Perez and Zerah. Tamar was their mother. Perez was the father of Hezron. Hezron was the father of Ram.
Ram was the father of Amminadab. Amminadab was the father of Nahshon. Nahshon was the father of Salmon.
Salem was the father of Boaz. Rahab was the mother of Boaz. Boaz was the father of Obed. Ruth was the mother of Obed. Obed was the father of Jesse.
And Jesse was the father of King David. And David was the father of Solomon. And Solomon's mother was the wife of Uriah.
Solomon was the father of Rehoboam. Rehoboam was the father of Abijah. Abijah was the father of Asa.
Asa was the father of Jehoshaphat. Jehoshaphat was the father of Joram. Joram was the father of Uzziah.
Azariah was the father of Jotham. Jotham was the father of Ahaz. Ahaz was the father of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah was the father of Manasseh. Manasseh was the father of Amon. Amon was the father of Josiah.
Josiah was the father of Jeconiah and his brothers. At that time, the Jewish people were forced to go to Babylon.
After that, the family line continued. Jeconiah was the father of Shealtiel. Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel was the father of Abiud. Abiud was the father of Eliakim. Eliakim was the father of Azor.
Azor was the father of Zadok. Zadok was the father of Ahitub. Ahitub was the father of Eliud.
Eleazar was the father of Eleazar. Eleazar was the father of Matan. Matan was the father of Jacob.
Jacob was the father of Joseph. Joseph was the husband of Mary. And Mary gave birth to Jesus, who is called the Christ.
From Abraham to David there were 14 generations. From David to the Babylonian exile there were 14 generations. From the Babylonian exile to Christ there were 14 generations. Jesus Christ was born.
Thus the birth of Jesus Christ came about. His mother was Mary, and Joseph had promised to marry her. But before they came together, it was discovered that she would conceive a child. She became pregnant by the power of the Holy Spirit.
Her husband Joseph was a God-fearing man. He did not wish to expose her publicly. So he planned to quietly divorce her.
But while Joseph was pondering this, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. The angel said, "Joseph, son of David, do not be afraid to take Mary into your home as your wife. For the child conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit."
She will give birth to a son. You shall name him Jesus. This is because he will save his people from their sins.
All this took place to fulfill what the Lord had said through the prophet: "The virgin will conceive and give birth to a son, and they will call him Immanuel."
The virgin will conceive and give birth to a son. And he will be called Immanuel." (Isaiah 7:14) The name Immanuel means "God with us."
Joseph awoke. He did what the angel of the Lord commanded him. He took Mary into his house as his wife.
But he knew her not until she had brought forth her firstborn son and he called his name Jesus.
The Visit of the Wise Men to Jesus
Jesus was born in Bethlehem in Judea. This happened while Herod was king of Judea. After Jesus was born, wise men from the east came to Jerusalem.
They asked, "Where is the child who was born to be king of the Jews? For we saw his star when it rose, and we have come to worship him."
When King Herod heard this, he was deeply disturbed, and everyone in Jerusalem was also disturbed.
Herod called together all the chief priests and the people's teachers. He asked them where the Messiah would be born.
"In Bethlehem of Judea," they answered him. "For thus it is written through the prophet:
"And you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, least of the clans of Judah, from you shall come forth to me the ruler of Israel, whose origin is from of old, from ancient times." (Micah 5:2)
Then Herod secretly called the wise men. He inquired of them diligently when the star appeared.
Send them to Bethlehem. He said, "Go! Search diligently for the child. As soon as you find him, bring me a report. Then I can go and worship him too."
After the wise men had listened to the king, they set out on their journey. The star that they had seen when they were in the east went on before them. And finally it stopped over the place where the child was.
When they saw the star, they were filled with joy.
The wise men went to the house. There they saw the child with his mother Mary. They bowed down and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures. They presented him with gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they returned to their country by another route. The flight of Jesus' family to Egypt
When the wise men left, Joseph dreamed. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him. The angel said, "Get up! Take the child and his mother and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you to return. Herod is going to look for the child. He wants to kill him."
Joseph got up. During the night, he left for Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
They remained there until King Herod died. Then the words spoken by the Lord through the prophet were fulfilled, for he had said, "Out of Egypt I called my son." (Hosea 11:1)
Herod realized that the wise men had deceived him. He became very angry. He issued orders concerning Bethlehem and the surrounding area. All the young boys who were two years old or less were to be killed. This was in accordance with the time that the wise men had seen the star.
In this way, the words spoken by the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. For he had said,
A voice is heard in Ramah, the voice of lamentation and deep mourning. Rachel weeps for her children. She refuses to be comforted, because they are gone. (Jeremiah 31:15) The Return of Jesus' Family to Nazareth
After Herod's death, Joseph dreamed while still in Egypt. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him.
The angel said, "Rise! Take the child and his mother. Go to the land of Israel. Those who were trying to kill the child have died."
Then Joseph arose, and took the child and his mother Mary, and returned into the land of Israel.
But he heard that Archelaus was king of Judea. Archelaus was ruling in place of his father Herod. This made Joseph afraid to go there. After he was warned in a dream, Joseph returned to the land of Galilee instead.
There he lived in a city called Nazareth. He fulfilled what the prophets had said about Jesus. For they had said, "He will be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
In those days John the Baptist came and preached in the wilderness of Judea.
He said, "Repent, for the Kingdom of Heaven is at hand."
John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke. For he said, "A voice is calling, 'In the wilderness prepare the way for the Lord; make straight paths for him in the desert.' " (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothes were made of camel hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
The people of Jerusalem and all of Judah went out to him. They also came from the entire region around the Jordan River.
When they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the Jordan River.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to where he was baptizing. He said to them, "You brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the wrath to come?
Bring forth fruits meet for repentance, showing that you have truly departed from your sins.
Do not think and say to yourselves, "Abraham is our father." I say to you, God is able to raise up children for Abraham even from these stones.
The axe is already at the roots of the trees. All the trees that do not bear good fruit will be cut down. They will be thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water, calling you to repent of your sins. But after me will come one who is mightier than I, and I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire.
His spear in his hand to remove the straw from his threshing floor. He will gather his wheat into the granary. But he will burn the straw with the unquenchable fire." Jesus is baptized
Jesus came from Galilee to the River Jordan. He wanted John to baptize him.
But John tried to prevent him. He said to Jesus, "I need to be baptized by you. So why are you coming to me?"
Jesus answered, "Let it be so now. It is right for us to do this. He is carrying out God's holy plan." Then John agreed.
As soon as Jesus was baptized, he came up out of the water. At that moment heaven was opened. Jesus saw the Spirit of God descending like a dove upon him.
And a voice from heaven said, "This is my Son, whom I love; with him I am well pleased."
Jesus is tempted.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the desert. There, Satan tempted him.
After forty days and forty nights of fasting, Jesus was hungry.
The tempter came to him and said, "If you are the Son of God, tell these stones to become bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written: 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that comes from the mouth of God.' " (Deuteronomy 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city. He made him stand on the highest point of the temple.
He said to him, "If you are the Son of God, throw yourself down. For it is written: 'He will command his angels concerning you, to guard you in all your ways. On their hands they will bear you up, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.' " (Psalm 91:11-12)
Jesus answered, "It is written, 'Do not test the Lord your God.'" (Deuteronomy 6:16)
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. He showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
He said, "If you fall down and prostrate to me, I will give you all of this."
Jesus said to him, "Get behind me, Satan! For it is written: 'Worship the Lord your God, and serve him only.'" (Deuteronomy 6:13)
Then the devil left Jesus. Angels came and took care of him. Jesus begins to preach.
John the Baptist was imprisoned. When Jesus heard about this, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth. He went to live in the city of Capernaum. It was located beside the lake in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
And in this way, there was fulfilled what the prophet Isaiah had said. He had said,
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! Galilee, where non-Jews live! The land along the Mediterranean Sea! The lands east of the Jordan River!
People who are now living in darkness will see a great light. They are now living in a very dark land. But a light will shine on them." (Isaiah 9:1, 2)
Since then, Jesus began to preach. He said, "Repent of your sins! For the Kingdom of Heaven is at hand." Jesus Chooses the First Disciples
One day Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw two brothers. They were Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Jesus said, "Come, follow me, and I will make you fishers of men."
So they left their nets and followed him immediately.
Advancing from there, he saw another two brothers. They were James the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in a boat with their father Zebedee. And as they were preparing their nets, Jesus called out to them.
Immediately they left the boat and their father and followed Jesus. Jesus heals the sick.
Jesus went throughout Galilee. There he taught in the synagogues. He preached the good news about the kingdom of God. He healed every disease and ailment that the people suffered from.
News about him spread throughout Syria. People brought him all the sick, those with various diseases. Some were suffering greatly from intense pains. Others were controlled by demons. Some were shaking violently. And others couldn't move at all. And Jesus healed them all.
Large crowds followed him. Some people came from Galilee, from the region known as the Decapolis, and from Jerusalem and Judea. Others came from the region located across the Jordan River.
Jesus bestows blessings.
Jesus saw the crowds. He went up on the mountain and sat down. His disciples came to him.
Then he began to teach them. He said,
Blessed are those who are spiritually needy, for the Kingdom of Heaven is theirs.
Blessed are those who mourn, for they will be comforted.
Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth.
Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they will be filled.
Blessed are the merciful, for they shall receive mercy.
Blessed are the pure in heart. They will see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called children of God.
Blessed are those who suffer for doing what is right. The Kingdom of Heaven belongs to them.
Blessed are you when men revile you and persecute you in my name. Blessed are you too when they call you all kinds of evil names because of me.
Rejoice and be glad. Your reward in heaven is great. In the same way, people hurt the prophets who lived long ago. Salt and light.
You are the salt of the earth. But suppose the salt loses its saltiness. How can it be made salty again? It is no longer good for anything, but is thrown out. People walk on it.
You are the light of the world. No one lights a lamp and puts it under a basket. Instead, they put it on a stand, where it gives light to everyone in the house.
Also, people don't light a lamp and then put it under a bowl. Instead, they put it on its stand. Then it illuminates everyone in the house.
In the same way, let your light shine before others. Then they will see the good things you do. And they will praise your Father in heaven. Jesus gives the full meaning of the law.
Do not think that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets. I have not come to abolish but to fulfill.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. Heaven and earth will pass away, but not the smallest letter or the smallest part of a letter will disappear from the Law until everything has happened.
Do not fail to observe even the least important commandments. And do not teach others to break them. If you do this, you will be called the least important in the Kingdom of Heaven. Rather, practice these commandments and teach them. Then you will be called important in the Kingdom of Heaven.
Here is what I say to you. You must be more righteous than the Pharisees and teachers of the law. If you are not, you will certainly not enter the kingdom of heaven. Murder
You have heard what was said to the people who lived long ago. It was said to them: "You must not kill." (Exodus 20:13) Whoever kills must be judged for that.
But this is what I'm telling you. Don't get angry with your brother. Whoever is angry with his brother will be brought before the court. And whoever says to his brother, 'You good-for-nothing!' must stand before the Sanhedrin for trial. But whoever says, 'You fool!' will be in danger of hellfire.
Assume that you are offering your gift at the altar. And remember that your brother has something against you.
Leave your gift in front of the altar. First, go and be reconciled with your brother. Then come back and offer your gift.
Suppose someone has a claim against you and takes you to court. Settle the matter quickly. Do it while he is with you on the way. If you don't, he may deliver you to the judge. The judge may deliver you to the officer. And you may be cast into prison.
What I will tell you is the truth. You will not leave until you pay the last penny! Adultery
You have heard that it was said, "Do not commit adultery." (Exodus 20:14)
But this is what I tell you: do not set your eyes on a woman in a lustful way. Whoever looks at a woman in this manner has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
If your right eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. Your eye is only a small part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your whole body thrown into hell.
If your right hand is tempting you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. Your hand is only one part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your whole body go to hell. Divorce.
It was said, "Suppose a man divorces his wife. If he does that, he must give her a divorce certificate." (Deuteronomy 24:1)
But this is what I am telling you. Whoever divorces his wife causes her to commit adultery; and whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery. A man is not to divorce his wife unless she is unfaithful to him. Oaths
Again, you have heard what was said to your people long ago. They were told, "Do not break the covenant that you made with the Lord your God. Keep the commandments that you were given, and the promises that you made to the Lord your God."
But that's what I'm telling you. Never make promises like that. Don't make promises in heaven's name. That's God's throne.
Do not make it in the name of the Earth. That is the seat for the feet of God. Do not make it in the name of Jerusalem. That is the city of the Great King.
And do not swear by your heads. You cannot make even one hair white or black.
Let your "yes" mean "yes," and your "no" mean "no." Anything more is from the evil one. Be kind to others.
You have heard that it was said, 'Eye for eye, and tooth for tooth.' (Exodus 21:24; Leviticus 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21)
But this is what I tell you. Do not fight against someone wicked. "Suppose someone strikes you on your right cheek. Then turn to him your other cheek also.
Assume someone is suing you to take your shirt. Let him take your coat as well.
If someone forces you to go one mile, go with him two miles.
Give to anyone who asks something of you. Do not refuse to lend something to anyone who wants to borrow something from you. Love your enemies.
Haven't you heard it said, 'Love your neighbor and hate your enemy'? (Leviticus 19:18)
But this is what I tell you. Love your enemies. Pray for those who hurt you.
You will be sons of your Father in heaven. "For he makes his sun rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the just and the unjust.
If you love those who love you, what reward will you get? Even the tax collectors do that.
And if you are greeted, then greet those who are better than you. Do not say, "How are you?" to a woman, for she is a private matter to you. And God is Knowing of what you do.
Be perfect, therefore, as your heavenly Father is perfect.
Giving to those in need.
Beware of doing "good works" before others. Do not do them to be seen by others. If you do that, your Father who is in heaven will not reward you.
When you give to the needy, do not announce it with trumpets. Do not be like those who pretend to be pious only. They announce what they do in the temples and in the streets. They want to be honored by others. What I am going to tell you is true. They have received their full reward.
When you give to the needy, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
You will secretly give your gift of grace, and your father sees, what you do secretly prayer
When you pray, do not be like the hypocrites. They love to stand and pray in the temples and on the street corners. They want to be seen by others. What I tell you is the truth. They have received their full reward.
When you pray, go into your room, and close the door and pray to your Father who is unseen. And He will reward you. For your Father sees what is done in secret.
When you pray, do not go on and on, like the evil people do. They think they will be heard because of their much speaking.
Don't be like them. Your father knows what you need even before you ask him.
This is how you should pray. "Our Father who art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name.
Let your kingdom come. Let your will be done on earth as it is in heaven.
Give us this day our daily bread.
Forgive us our trespasses, as we also forgive those who trespass against us.
Deliver us from the evil one. Lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil.
Forgive others when they wrong you. If you do this, your Father who is in heaven will also forgive you.
But if you do not forgive men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses. Fasting.
When you fast, you don't look as gloomy as those who pretend to be pious only. They make their faces look very sad. They want to show people that they are fasting. What I am going to tell you is the truth. They have received their full reward.
But when you go out without eating, put olive oil on your head. Wash your face.
Then the others will not know that you are fasting. Only your unseen Father will know that. He will reward you. Your Father sees what is done in secret. Lay up treasures in heaven
Do not store up for yourselves treasures on earth, where moth and rust destroy, and where thieves break in and steal.
Instead, lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust destroys, and where thieves do not break in or steal.
Your heart will be where your treasures are.
The eye is like a lamp for the body. Suppose your eyes are good. Then your whole body will be filled with light.
But suppose your eyes are bad. Then your whole body will be full of darkness. If the light within you is darkness, then it will be very dark!
No one can serve two masters at the same time. Either he will hate one and love the other, or he will be devoted to one and hate the other. You cannot serve both God and money. Do not worry.
I tell you, do not be worried. Do not be worried about your life and what you will eat or drink. And do not be worried about your bodies and what you will wear. Is there not more to life than eating? Are there not greater things for the body than clothing?
Look at the birds of the air. For they neither sow nor reap, nor gather into barns. Yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not much better than they?
Can you add just one hour to your life by worrying?
And why do you worry about clothing? Look at the wild flowers. They do not work, they do not make clothes.
But that is what I am telling you. Even Solomon in all his glory was not arrayed like one of those flowers.
If God clothes the wild grass like this, will He not clothe you even better? For the grass is here today only, and tomorrow it is thrown into the fire. Your faith is very small!
Therefore do not be anxious. And do not say, "What will we eat?" or "What will we drink?" or "What will we wear?"
People who don't fear God run after all those things. Your Father who is in heaven knows that you need them.
But first seek the kingdom of God. Do what He wants you to do. Then all those other things will be given to you as well.
So do not worry about tomorrow. Tomorrow will worry about itself. Each day has enough of its own problems.
Be fair when you judge others.
Do not judge others, and you will not be judged.
You will be judged as you judge others. You will be measured as you measure others.
You look at the sawdust speck in your friend's eye. But you do not notice the wooden beam in your own eye.
How can you say to your friend, "Let me take the splinter out of your eye"? How can you say this when there is a piece of wood in your own eye?
Hey demonstrator! First, take the plank out of your own eye. Then you will be able to see clearly to take the splinter out of your friend's eye.
Do not cast your pearls before swine. Do not give what is holy to dogs. If you do, they may turn and tear you to pieces. Ask, seek, knock
Ask and it will be given to you. Seek and you will find. Knock and the door will be opened for you.
To him who asks shall be given. To him who knocks shall be opened. Seek and you will find. Knock and the door will be opened to you.
If your son asks you for bread, which of you would give him a stone?
Or suppose he asked for a fish. Which of you would give him a snake?
And if you are evil, know how to give good gifts to your children. How much more will your Father in heaven give good gifts to those who ask him!
In everything, treat others as you would like to be treated. This is what is written in the Torah and the Prophets. The great and small gates.
Enter the Kingdom of God through the narrow gate. The gate is wide and the road is broad that leads to destruction and hell. Many people are walking down that road.
But the gate is narrow and the road is narrow that leads to life. Few are those who find it. A tree and its fruit.
Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.
You can know their true character by what they do. Do people pick grapes from thorny bushes? Do they pick figs from thistles?
In the same way, every good tree bears good fruit, but a bad tree bears bad fruit.
A good tree does not bear evil fruit. And an evil tree does not bear good fruit.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down. It is thrown into the fire.
You can know every tree by its fruit.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only the one who does the will of my Father who is in heaven.
Many will say to me on that day: 'Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name? Did we not cast out demons in your name? Did we not do mighty works in your name?'
Then I will tell them plainly, "I never knew you. Away from me, you evildoers!" The builders, the wise and the foolish.
Therefore, whoever hears my words and does them will be like a wise man who built his house on the rock.
The rain comes. The water rises. The wind blows and beats against that house. But it does not fall. It is built on the rock.
But everyone who hears my words and does not do them is like a foolish man who built his house on sand.
The rain comes. The water rises. The wind blows and hits that house. And it falls with a loud sound.
Jesus finished saying all these things. The crowd was amazed at his teaching.
He taught as one having authority. He did not speak as the teachers of the law.
Jesus heals a man who was suffering from a skin disease.
Jesus descended from the mountainside. A great crowd followed him.
A man who was suffering from a skin disease came and knelt before Jesus. He said, "Lord, if you are willing, you can make me clean."
Jesus stretched out his hand and touched the man. He said, "I am willing to do so." "Be clean!" Immediately the man was healed from his skin disease.
Then Jesus said to him, "Do not tell anyone. Go and show yourself to the priest. Offer the gift that Moses commanded. This will be a testimony to them." A Roman Centurion Believes
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman centurion came to him. He asked Jesus for help.
He said, "Oh Lord, my servant is lying in the house and cannot move. He is suffering greatly."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
The captain replied, "Master, I am not worthy to have you come under my roof. But just say the word, and my servant will be healed.
I myself am a man under authority. And I have soldiers who obey my orders. I say to one, "Go," and he goes. And I say to another, "Come," and he comes. And I say to my servant, "Do this," and he does it.
When Jesus heard this, he was amazed. He said to those who were following him, "What I am about to tell you is true. In Israel, I have not found anyone whose faith is as strong as this."
I tell you that many will come from the east and the west. They will take their places at the feast in the kingdom of heaven. They will be seated with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.
But as for those who think that they belong to the kingdom, they will be thrown outside, into the darkness. There they will weep and grind their teeth."
Then Jesus said to the Roman leader, "Go! It will be as you have believed." And his servant was healed at that very hour. Jesus heals many people
When Jesus entered Peter's house, he saw Peter's mother-in-law. She was lying down on the bed. She was suffering from a fever.
Jesus touched her hand, and the fever left her. She rose and began to serve him.
When evening came, many possessed by demons were brought to Jesus. He cast out the spirits with a word. He healed all the sick.
He did this to fulfill what the prophet Isaiah had said: "He was wounded for our transgressions, and was bruised for our iniquities. Upon him was the chastisement that made us whole, and by his stripes we are healed." (Isaiah 53:4,5) The cost of following Jesus.
Jesus saw the crowd around him. He told his disciples to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee.
Then the teacher of the law came to him and said, "Teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
Jesus answered, "The foxes have holes and the birds of the air have nests, but the Son of Man has no place to lay his head."
Another disciple said to him, "Lord, let me first go and bury my father."
Jesus said to him, "Follow me. Let the dead bury their dead." Jesus Calms the Storm
Jesus climbed into the boat. His disciples followed him.
Suddenly a terrible storm came upon the lake. The waves beat against the boat. But Jesus was sleeping.
The disciples went and woke him up. "Lord!" they said. "Save us! We're going to drown!"
He answered, "It is because your faith is so small! Why are you so afraid?" Then Jesus got up and rebuked the winds and the waves, and they stopped, and there was a calm.
The students were amazed. They asked, "Who is this man, that even the winds and the waves obey him?" Jesus heals two men who were controlled by demons.
Jesus arrived on the other side of the lake, in the region of the Gadarenes. Two men who were possessed by demons met him. They had come from the tombs. The men were so violent that no one could travel on that road.
"Son of God, what do you want from us?" they cried out. "Have you come here to punish us before it is time for our judgment?"
Not very far away, a large herd of pigs was grazing.
The demons pleaded with Jesus, "If you drive us out, send us into the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go!" So the demons came out of the men and entered the pigs. The entire herd rushed down the steep slope. They ran to the lake and drowned in the water.
The men who tended the pigs fled. They went into the city and reported all this to everyone. They told the people what had happened to the men who were controlled by the demons.
Then the whole city went out to meet Jesus. When they saw him, they begged him to leave their region.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
Jesus got into the boat. He crossed to the other side of the lake and reached his own town.
Some men brought a man to Jesus who could not walk. He was lying on a mat. Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man, "Do not lose heart, my son. Your sins are forgiven."
Then some teachers of the law said to themselves, "This man is saying an evil thing!"
Jesus knew what they were thinking. And he said, "Why do you harbor evil thoughts in your hearts?"
Is it easier to say, "Your sins are forgiven"? or to say, "Rise and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then He said to the man who could not walk, "Get up," He said. "Take your mat and go to your house."
The man got up and went home.
When the crowd saw this, they were filled with awe. And they praised God, who had given such authority to human beings. Jesus chooses Matthew
And as Jesus went on from there, he saw a man called Matthew sitting at the tax collector's booth. Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And rising up, Matthew followed him.
Later, Jesus was having dinner at Matthew's house. Many tax collectors and "sinners" came and ate with Jesus and his disciples.
The Pharisees saw this. And they asked the disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard this. And He said, "Those who are well do not need a physician, but those who are ill do."
Go and learn what this means: "I desire mercy, not sacrifice." (Hosea 6:6) I did not come to call those who think they are righteous with God to follow me. I came to call sinners to follow me." Jesus is asked about fasting.
One day, the disciples of John the Baptist came. They said to Jesus, "We and the Pharisees fast, but why do not your disciples fast?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom mourn while he is with them? The time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them; then they will fast.
People do not patch old clothes with new cloth. The new piece will tear away from the old. This will make the tear worse.
A dead girl and a suffering woman.
While Jesus was saying this, a ruler came and knelt down before Jesus. He said, "My daughter has just died. But come and put your hand on her, and she will live again."
Jesus rose up and went with him. And so did his disciples.
At that moment, a woman came from behind Jesus. She had a disease that made her bleed. This had been going on for 12 years. She touched the edge of his clothes.
I thought, "I just need to touch his clothes. Then I will be healed."
Jesus turned and saw her. He said, "Woman, you have great faith; don't lose heart." Her faith made her well, and she was healed at that very moment.
When Jesus entered the ruler's house, he saw the flute players there. And he saw the noisy crowd.
He said, "Go. The girl is not dead. She is sleeping." But they laughed at him.
After the crowd was sent outside, Jesus entered. He took the hand of the girl, and she arose.
News of what Jesus had done spread throughout the whole region. Jesus heals two blind men.
As Jesus was leaving there, two blind men followed him. They called out to him, "Have mercy on us, O Son of David!"
When Jesus went inside, the blind people came to him. He asked them, "Do you believe that I can do this?" "Yes, Lord," they answered.
Then he touched their eyes. He said, "It will happen to you as you have believed."
They could now see again. Jesus warned them severely saying, "Be sure that no one knows about this."
But they went out and spread the news. They talked about it all over that region.
As they were coming out, another man was brought to Jesus. An evil spirit controlled him, and he could not speak.
When the devil was cast out, the man spoke. The crowd was astonished. They said, "Nothing like this has ever been seen in Israel."
But the Pharisees said, "He casts out demons by the prince of demons." There are only a few laborers
Jesus was going throughout all the cities and villages. He was teaching in their synagogues, preaching the good news of the kingdom, and healing every disease and sickness.
When he saw the crowds, he felt a deep concern for them. They were exhausted and helpless, like sheep without a shepherd.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "The harvest is plentiful, but the laborers are few."
Ask the Lord of the harvest to send out workers into his harvest field.
Jesus Sends Out the Twelve Disciples.
Jesus called his twelve disciples to come to him. He gave them authority to drive out demons and to heal every disease and illness.
Here are the names of the twelve apostles. The first of them is Simon Peter, and his brother Andrew. Then comes James, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John.
The following are Philips and Bartholomew, as well as Thomas and Matthew the tax collector. And there are two others, James the son of Alphaeus, and Thaddaeus.
The last two are Simon the Zealot and Judas Iscariot. Judas is the one who was later going to hand Jesus over to his enemies.
Jesus sent these twelve out with the following instructions. He said, "Do not go to those who are not Jews." "Do not enter any city of the Samaritans."
Go instead to the children of Israel. For they are like sheep that have gone astray.
As you go, preach, saying, "The kingdom of heaven is at hand."
Heal the sick. Raise the dead. Make those suffering from skin diseases "pure" again. Expel demons. You received freely, so give freely.
Do not take gold or silver or bronze in your belts.
Do not take a bag for the trip. Do not take extra clothes, sandals, or walking sticks. The worker should be given what they need.
When you enter a town or village, look for someone willing to receive you. Stay at that person's home until you leave.
Upon entering the house, greet those who live there.
If that house receives you, give it the blessing of peace. And if it does not, do not bless it.
Some people may not welcome you or listen to your words. And if they do not, shake the dust of your feet when you leave that house or city.
What I am going to tell you is the truth. On the Day of Judgment, the fate of Sodom and Gomorrah will be easier than the fate of that city.
I send you out as sheep among wolves. Therefore be wise as serpents and harmless as doves.
Beware! The men will deliver you to the local courts. They will flog you in their temples.
You will be brought before governors and kings for my sake. You will be witnesses for them and for those who are not Jews.
But when they arrest you, don't worry about what you will say or how you will say it. At that time, the right words will be given to you to say.
It will not be you who speaks. The spirit of your father is what will speak through you.
Brothers will deliver their brothers to be killed. Fathers will deliver their children. Children will rise up against their fathers and execute them.
Everyone will hate you because of me. But whoever endures to the end will be saved.
When people attack you in one place, flee to another. What I will tell you is true. You will not finish going through the cities of Israel before the Son of Man comes.
The student is no better than his teacher. The servant is no better than his master.
It is enough for a student to be like their teacher, and it is enough for a servant to be like their master. If the master of the house is called Beelzebul, what can those others who live there expect?
Do not fear your enemies. What is secret will be revealed to the public. What is hidden will be shown.
What I tell you in the dark, speak in the light. What is whispered in your ear, shout from the rooftops.
Fear not those who kill the body and cannot kill the soul; rather, fear the One who is able to destroy both soul and body in hell.
Are not two sparrows sold for a farthing? And one of them shall not fall on the ground without your Father.
He is counting even every hair on your head!
Do not be afraid. You are worth more than many sparrows.
What about someone who says in front of others that they know me? I will also say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I know him.
But what about someone who says in front of others that he does not know me? I will say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I do not know him.
Do not suppose that I have come to bring peace to the earth. I did not come to bring peace, but a sword.
I have come to make sons rise up against their fathers and daughters against their mothers, and a daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law.
"The enemies of a man are the men of his own house." (Micah 7:6)
He who loves his father or mother more than me is not worthy of me. He who loves his son or daughter more than me is not worthy of me.
He who does not bear his cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
If anyone finds his life, he will lose it. And if anyone loses his life for me, he will find it.
He who receives you receives me, and he who receives me receives the one who sent me.
Assume that someone greets a prophet as a prophet. That person will receive the reward of a prophet. And assume that someone greets a pious person as a pious person. That person will receive the reward of a pious person.
Suppose someone gave a child who follows me a glass of cold water. What I am going to tell you is true. That person will certainly be rewarded.
Jesus and John the Baptist
Jesus finished teaching his twelve disciples. Then he continued teaching and preaching in the cities of Galilee.
John was in prison. When he heard what Christ was doing, he sent his disciples to him.
They asked Jesus, "Are you the one who was to come, or shall we look for another?"
Jesus answered, "Go back to John. Tell him what you hear and what you see.
The blind receive their sight. The lame walk. Those with skin diseases are healed. The deaf hear. The dead are raised to life. And the good news is preached to the poor.
Blessed are those who do not renounce their faith for me.
While John's disciples were leaving, Jesus began to speak to the crowd about John. He said, "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed shaken by the wind?"
If not, what did you go out to see? A man dressed in fine clothes? No. People who dress in fine clothes are in the palaces of kings.
What then did you go out to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet.
He is the one about whom it was written in the Holy Book. It says: "Behold, I will send my messenger, and he will prepare the way before you." (Malachi 3:1)
What I am going to tell you is the truth. No one has ever been born who is greater than John the Baptist. But the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven is greater than he is.
For John the Baptist's days, the Kingdom of the Heavens was advancing forcefully. And powerful people are those who seize it.
All the prophets and the law prophesied until John came.
If you are willing to accept that, then John is Elijah who was supposed to come.
Let him who has ears hear.
What can I compare the people of today with? They are like children sitting in the markets calling out to others. They say,
We played the flute for you. But you did not dance. We sang a funeral march. But you were not sad.
When John came, he did not eat or drink, and you say, "He has a demon."
But when the Son of Man came, he ate and drank as you do. And people say, "This man is a glutton and a drunkard, and a friend of tax collectors and sinners." But wisdom is justified by her deeds." The cities that do not repent of their sins
Jesus began to speak out against the cities where He had performed most of His miracles. The people there had not turned from their sins. And He said,
Woe to you, Korazin! And woe to you, Bethsaida! For if the miracles had been performed in Tyre and Sidon which were performed in you, they would have repented long ago in sackcloth and ashes. But instead, they continued to wear black clothing. And they sat on the ash.
But I tell you this. On the day of judgment, Tyre and Sidon will be easier to endure than you will be.
And what about you, Capernaum? Will you be exalted to heaven? No! You will be brought down to the place of the dead. Suppose the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Sodom; it would have remained until this day.
But I tell you this. On the day of judgment, the fate of Sodom will be easier than your fate." Comfort for all who are weary.
At that time Jesus said, "I thank you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because you have hidden these things from the wise and learned and revealed them to infants. Yes, Father, for this is how you wanted it.
Yes, Dad. This is what I wanted.
The Father gave me everything. The Father is the only one who knows the Son. And the only ones who know the Father are the Son and those whom the Son chooses to make known to him.
Come to me, all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest.
Become my slaves and learn from me. For I am gentle and humble in heart. And you will find rest for your souls.
"My service is easy, and my burden is light."
Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
On a Sabbath, Jesus was walking through the grainfields. His disciples were hungry. So they began to pluck some heads of grain and eat them.
The Pharisees saw this; and they said to Jesus, "Look! This man is doing what is unlawful on the Sabbath! But your disciples are doing this too!"
Jesus answered, "Haven't you read what David did when he and his men were hungry?
He entered the house of God. He and his men ate the holy bread. Only the priests were allowed to eat it.
Have you not read the law? It is written how the priests in the temple should do their work every Sabbath day. But it is not considered they have sinned.
I tell you that the one who is greater than the temple is here.
The writings say, "I desire mercy, and not sacrifice." (Hosea 6:6) You do not know what these words mean. If you did know, you would not bring accusations against those who are innocent.
The Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath.
He left that place and entered their synagogue.
There was a man who had a withered and crooked hand. The Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. So they asked him, "Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?"
He said to them, "What if one of your sheep falls into a pit on the Sabbath? Wouldn't you hold it and lift it out?"
Man is of a higher order than the beasts! Therefore, the law permits us to do good on the Sabbath.
Then Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." And he stretched it out; and it was restored, like as the other.
But the Pharisees went out and conspired to kill Jesus, the chosen servant of God.
Jesus knew all about the Pharisees' plans. So he left that place. Many people followed him, and he healed all their sick.
But he warned them not to tell who it was.
That was to fulfill what was spoken by the prophet Isaiah, who said,
Here is my servant, whom I uphold, my chosen one in whom I delight. I will put my Spirit on him, and he will announce to the nations that every thing will be made new.
He will not argue or shout. No one will hear his voice in the streets.
He will not break a bent branch. He will not extinguish a flame that is dimming its light. He will make everything alright.
Nations shall put their hope in him.
A man possessed by demons was brought to Jesus. The man was blind and unable to speak. Jesus healed this man. Then the man was able to speak and see.
All the people were amazed. And they said, "Is this the Son of David?"
When the Pharisees heard this, they said, "This fellow is not casting out demons except by Beelzebul, the ruler of the demons."
Jesus knew their thoughts. He said to them, "Every kingdom divided against itself is ruined. And every city or family divided against itself will not stand."
If Satan casts out Satan, he is at war with himself. So how can his kingdom stand?
You say that I cast out demons by the power of Beelzebul. By whose power, then, do *your* people cast out demons? Therefore, they will be the judges over you.
But suppose I cast out demons by the Spirit of God. Then the kingdom of God has come upon you.
Or consider this. How can you enter a powerful man's house and just take what he owns? You must first tie him up. Then you can rob his house.
He who is not with me is against me. And he who does not gather the sheep with me, scatters them.
Here is what I say to you. Every sin and every evil word spoken against God will be forgiven. But speaking evil things against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven.
Everyone who speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven. But whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven, either in this age or in the age to come.
If you make the tree good, its fruit will be good. If you make the tree bad, its fruit will be bad. You can know the tree by its fruit.
O nests of poisonous vipers! How can you evil ones say anything good? Out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaks.
A good man says good things. These come from the goodness which is stored up inside him. An evil man says evil things. These come from the evil which is stored up inside him.
But that is what I am telling you. On the Day of Judgment, people will be held accountable for every careless word they spoke.
"By your words you will be condemned or acquitted." The miraculous sign of Jonah
Some Pharisees and teachers of the law came to Jesus and said, "Teacher, we want to see a miraculous sign from you."
He answered, "Evil and unfaithful people seek a sign! But no sign will be given except the sign of Jonah."
Jonah was in the belly of a great fish for three days and three nights. Something similar will happen to the Son of Man. He will spend three days and three nights in the tomb.
The men of Nineveh will stand on the day of judgment with the people who are now living. And the men of Nineveh will prove that these people are guilty. The men of Nineveh turned away from their sins when Jonah preached to them. And now there is someone greater than Jonah.
The Queen of the South will rise on the Last Day with the people who are living now. And she will testify that they are guilty. She came from a very long way to hear the wisdom of Solomon. And now there is someone greater than Solomon here.
What happens when an evil spirit comes out of a person? It passes through dry regions, searching for a place of rest. But it does not find it.
Then he says, "I will go back to the house I left." And when he gets there, he finds the house empty. The house has been cleaned and tidied.
Then the evil spirit goes out and takes with it seven other evil spirits more wicked than itself. They enter and dwell there. The man becomes worse off than before. This is how the wicked will be in these times." Jesus' mother and his brothers
While Jesus was still talking to the crowd, his mother and brothers stood outside. They wanted to talk to him.
Someone told him: "Your mother and brothers are standing outside. They want to talk to you."
Jesus answered and said to him, "Who is My mother? And who are My brethren?"
Jesus pointed to his disciples. He said, "This is my mother! These are my brothers!"
Whoever does the will of my Father who is in heaven, he is my brother and sister and mother.
The Farmer's Story
On that same day Jesus went out of the house and sat by the Sea of Galilee.
A large crowd gathered around him. So he got into a boat. He sat in it. All the people stood on the shore.
Then he told them many things using parables. He said, "A farmer went out to sow his seed.
Scattering the seeds on the ground. Some fell on the road. The birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell on rocky places, where there wasn't much soil. The plants sprouted quickly, because the soil wasn't deep.
When the sun rose, it burned the plants. They dried up because they had no roots.
Some seeds fell among the thorns. The thorns grew and the plants became crowded.
Other seed fell on good soil. It produced a crop that was a hundred, sixty or thirty times what was planted.
Let him who has ears hear.
The disciples came to him. They asked, "Why do you use stories when you talk to people?"
He answered, "I have been given the opportunity to understand the secrets of the Kingdom of Heaven. They were not given to outsiders."
Everyone who possesses that type of knowledge will be given more. Indeed, he will have a very great deal. And whoever does not possess that type of knowledge, even the little that he has will be taken from him.
Here's why I use stories when I talk to people. I say: "They are looking, but they don't really see. They are listening, but they don't really hear or understand."
In them the words of the prophet Isaiah are fulfilled. He said: "You will indeed hear but never understand; and you will indeed see but never perceive.
These people's hearts are hardened. They barely hear with their ears. They have closed their eyes. Otherwise, they might see with their eyes. They might hear with their ears. They might understand with their hearts. They might return to the Lord, and then he would heal them.' (Isaiah 6:9, 10)
But blessed are your eyes, for they see; and your ears, for they hear.
What I will tell you is the truth. Many prophets and righteous people wanted to see what you see. But they did not see it. They wanted to hear what you hear. But they did not hear it.
Listen! This is the meaning of the peasant's story.
People hear the message about the Kingdom, but they do not understand it. Then the evil one comes. He steals what was sown in their hearts. These people are like seed sown along the path.
And those whose seeds were cut off on the rocky ground, they are those who hear the word and rejoice at it for a while.
But they have no roots, so they last only a short time. They quickly fall away from the faith when troubles or suffering come because of the message.
And those who received the seed among the thorns are those who hear the word, but the anxieties of this life and the deceitfulness of wealth choke it, and it bears no fruit.
But others, they received the seed that fell on good soil. They are the ones who hear the message and understand it. They produce a crop a hundred, sixty, or thirty times what the farmer planted." The Parable of the Weeds
Jesus told the crowd another parable. "Thus is the Kingdom of Heaven," He said. "A man sowed good seeds in his field.
But while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then left.
The wheat started to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
The owner's servants came to him. They said, "Sir, did you not sow good seeds in your field? Where, then, have the weeds come from?"
He said, "An enemy has done this." The servants asked him, "Do you want us to go and pull out the weeds?"
"No," the owner replied. "While you are pulling out the weeds, you might pull up the wheat along with them."
Both of them prayed that they would grow together until the harvest. And when that time came, I would tell the workers what they should do." This is what I would say to them: "First, gather the harmful weeds. Tie them into bundles to be burned. Then, gather the wheat. Bring it into my storage." From: The Mustard Seed and the Yeast: Inspirational Stories for Challenging Times, by Wayne Dyer (1996)
Jesus said to the crowd another parable. He said, "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed. Someone took the seed and planted it in a field.
It is the smallest of all your seeds. But when it grows, it becomes the largest plant in the whole garden. It becomes a tree. The birds come and rest on its branches.
Jesus told them another parable. He said, "The kingdom of heaven is like yeast," "a woman who mixed it into a large amount of flour. The yeast spread through the entire dough."
Jesus spoke all these things to the crowds in parables. He did not speak to them without a parable.
The words which the Prophet spoke proved true. He said, "I open my mouth in a parable, I declare mysterious things from of old. I speak of things hidden from the creation of the world." (Psalm 78:2) Jesus explains the parable of the evil seed.
Then Jesus left the crowd and went into the house. His disciples came to him. They said, "Explain to us the parable of the weeds in the field."
He answered, "The one who sowed the good seed is the son of man.
The field is the world. The good seed represents the people who belong to the kingdom. The weeds are the people who belong to the evil one.
The enemy who plants them is the devil. The harvest is the Day of Resurrection. And the laborers are the angels.
The weeds are uprooted and burned in the fire. So it will be on the day of judgment.
The Son of Man will send his angels. They will uproot from his kingdom everything that causes sin. They will also destroy everyone who does evil.
They will throw them into the blazing furnace. There they will wail and grind their teeth.
Then the people of God will shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Whoever has ears to hear, let them hear. Tales of the Hidden Treasure and the Pearl
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field. When a man found it, he hid it again, and out of joy went and sold all that he had and bought that field.
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant seeking fine pearls.
He found one that was very precious. So he went and sold everything he owned. And he bought that pearl. The Parable of the Net.
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net. It was cast into the lake. It gathered every kind of fish.
When it was full, the fishermen drew the net to the beach. Then they sat down and gathered the good fish into baskets. But they cast the bad fish away.
This is how it will be on the Day of Judgment. The angels will come. They will separate the people who did what is wrong from those who did what is right.
They will throw the evil persons into the fiery foundry. There the evildoers will weep and grind their teeth.
Jesus asked, "Do you understand all these things?" They answered, "Yes."
He told them, "Every scribe who is instructed in the kingdom of heaven is like a master of a house; he brings out of his storeroom treasures new and old." A prophet without honor.
Jesus finished telling these stories. Then he moved on from there.
He came to his hometown of Nazareth. There he began to teach people in their synagogue. They were amazed. "Where did this man get this wisdom? And where did he get this power to perform miracles?" they asked.
Is not this the son of the carpenter? Are not his mother's name Mary? Are not his brothers James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?
Aren't all his sisters with us? So where did this man get all these things from?
They were not pleased with him at all. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor except in his own town and in his own home."
He only performed a few miracles there because they did not have faith.
John the Baptist's head was cut off.
At that time Herod Antipas, the ruler of Galilee and Perea, heard reports about Jesus.
He told his entourage, "This is John the Baptist. He has risen from the dead! That is why he has the power to perform miracles."
Herod had arrested John, bound him, and put him in prison because of Herodias. She was the wife of Philip, the brother of Herod.
John was telling Herod, "It is against the law to take her."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people, because they thought that John was a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias's daughter danced for Herod and his guests. She pleased Herod greatly.
So he promised her with an oath that he would give her everything she asked for.
Her mother told her what to say. And the girl said to Herod, "Give me the head of John the Baptist on a platter."
The king was very upset. But he remembered his promise and his dinner guests. So he ordered one of his men to give her what she asked for.
Herod put John the Baptist to death in prison.
His head was brought on a large plate and given to the girl. Then she carried it to her mother.
John's disciples came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds the five thousand.
Jesus heard what had happened to John. He wanted to be alone. So he went in a boat to a quiet place. The crowd heard about this. They followed him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus arrived at the shore, he saw a large crowd. He felt deep concern for them. He healed their sick.
As evening approached, the disciples came to Him. They said, "There is nothing here. It is already late. Send the crowds away to go into the villages and buy something for themselves."
Jesus answered, "They do not need to go away. You give them something to eat."
They said, "We have only five loaves of bread and two fish."
"Bring them here to me," he said.
Then Jesus instructed the people to sit on the grass. He took the five loaves and the two fish. He looked up to heaven and thanked Him. He broke the loaves into pieces. Then He gave them to the disciples. And the disciples gave them to the people.
They all ate and were filled. The disciples gathered twelve baskets full of the leftover pieces.
There were about 5,000 men who ate. Women and children also ate. Jesus walks on the water.
Immediately Jesus made the disciples get into the boat. And he commanded them to go before him to the other side of the Sea of Galilee. Then he sent the multitude away.
After he sent them away, he went up by himself into a mountain to pray. And when evening came, he was there alone.
The boat was already far from land. The waves were beating it hard because the wind was blowing against it.
Early in the morning, Jesus went out to the disciples. He walked on the lake.
They saw him walking on the lake, so they were afraid. They said, "He is a ghost!" and they screamed with fear.
Immediately Jesus called out to them, saying, "Take heart! I am he. Do not be afraid."
Peter said, "Lord, if it is you, tell me to come to you on the water." Jesus said, "Come." So Peter got out of the boat and walked on the water to Jesus.
Jesus said, "Come." So Peter got out of the boat. He walked on the water toward Jesus.
But when Peter saw the wind, he was afraid. He began to sink. He cried out, "Lord, save me!"
Immediately Jesus reached out his hand and caught him. "Your faith is so small!" he said. "Why did you doubt me?"
When they climbed into the boat, the wind died down.
Then those in the boat worshiped Jesus. They said, "You are truly the Son of God!"
They crossed the lake and disembarked at Gennesaret.
The men who lived there recognized Jesus. So they sent a message into all the surrounding countryside. People brought all their sick to Jesus.
They begged him to let the sick people just touch the edge of his clothes. And whoever touched him was healed.
What makes people "unclean"?
Some Pharisees and some teachers of the law came from Jerusalem to see Jesus. And they asked,
Why do your disciples not obey what the elders teach? Your disciples do not wash their hands before they eat!
Jesus answered, "Why do you not obey God's command? Instead, you prefer to follow your own teachings!"
God said, "Honor your father and your mother." (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16) He also said, " 'If anyone curses his father or his mother, he will be put to death.' " (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9)
But you allow people to say to their parents: "Any help you may have received from us is a gift intended for God."
They do not need to honor their parents with their gift. You make the word of God of no effect, following your own traditions.
O protesters! Isaiah was right when he prophesied about you. He said,
These people honor me with their words, but their hearts are far from me.
Their worship means nothing to me. They teach only man-made rules." (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the crowd to Him. He said, "Listen, and understand."
What enters your mouth does not make you "unclean." What comes out of your mouth is what makes you "unclean."
Then the disciples came to him. They asked, "Do you know that the Pharisees were angered when they heard this?"
Jesus answered, "There are plants my Father in heaven did not plant; these will be pulled up by the roots.
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind man leads a blind man, both will fall into a pit.
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
Jesus asked them, "Do you not understand yet?"
Don't you see? Everything that enters the mouth goes to the stomach. Then it comes out of the body.
But the things that come out of the mouth come from the heart. Those are the things that make you "unclean."
Evil thoughts proceed from the heart. So also murder, adultery, and sexual immorality in general. Also theft, false witness, and lying to one another.
Those are the things that make you "unclean." But eating without washing your hands does not make you "unclean." "The Faith of a Canaanite Woman"
Jesus left Galilee and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
A woman from Canaan who lived near Tyre and Sidon came to him and cried out, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on me! A demon has seized my daughter. She suffers greatly."
Jesus did not say a single word. His disciples came near to him. They begged him, "Send her away. She keeps crying out after us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel."
Then the woman knelt before him. She said, "Oh Lord! Help me!"
He answered, "It is not right to take the children's bread and throw it to the dogs."
"Yes, Lord," she said. "But even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their masters' tables."
Jesus answered, "Woman, your faith is great! What you ask for will be done." And her daughter was healed at that moment. Jesus Feeds the Four Thousand
Jesus left that place. He walked along the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up on the side of a mountain and sat down.
Large crowds gathered around him. They brought to him blind people and those who could not walk. They also brought the crippled, those who could not speak, and many others. They placed them at his feet, and he healed them.
The people were amazed. Those who could not speak began to speak. Those who were crippled were healed. Those who could not walk began to walk. Those who were blind gained their sight. And the people praised the God of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples to come to him. He said, "I am deeply concerned about these people. They have been with me for three days now. They have nothing to eat. I don't want to send them away hungry. If I do, they will become too weak to make it back home."
His disciples answered him, "There is nothing here," they said. "Where can we get enough bread to feed this large crowd?"
Jesus asked, "How many loaves do you have?" They replied, "Seven, and a few small fish."
Jesus told the crowd to sit on the ground.
Taking the seven loaves and the fish, He gave thanks. Then He broke them and gave them to the disciples, and the disciples distributed them to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. After that, the disciples collected seven baskets of leftover pieces.
The number of men who ate was 40,000. Women and children also ate.
After Jesus sent the crowd away, he got into the boat. He went to the region near Magdala.
Jesus is asked for a miraculous sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a miraculous sign from heaven.
He answered, "In the evening you look at the sky. You say, 'It will be fine weather. The sky is red.'"
And in the morning you say, "Today will be stormy. The sky is red and cloudy." You know what you see in the sky means. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
"A wicked and adulterous generation seeks for a sign; but no sign shall be given to it, except the sign of the prophet Jonah." And Jesus left them and went away. The leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The pupils crossed to the other side of the lake. They had forgotten to take bread.
Jesus said to them, "Watch out for the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The pupils talked about this among themselves. They said: "He must be saying this because we did not bring any bread."
Jesus knew what they were saying. And he said, "Your faith is very small! Why are you talking to each other about there being no bread?
Don't you understand yet? Don't you remember the five loaves for the five thousand? Don't you remember how many baskets of fragments you collected?
Do you not remember the seven loaves for the four thousand? Do you not remember how many baskets of fragments you gathered?
How is it that you do not understand? I was not speaking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
Then the disciples understood that Jesus was not telling them to beware of the yeast used in bread. Rather, He was warning them about what the Pharisees and Sadducees taught. Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus went into the region of Caesarea Philippi. There he asked his disciples, "Who do people say the Son of Man is?"
And they answered, "Some say John the Baptist; some Elijah; and others Jeremiah, or one of the prophets."
"But what about you?" he asked. "Who do you say that I am?"
Peter answered and said: "You are the Christ. You are the Son of the Living God."
Jesus answered, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! For this was not revealed to you by flesh and blood, but by My Father who is in heaven.
Here is what I say to you. You are Peter. On this rock I will build my church. The gates of hell will not be strong enough to destroy it.
I will give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven. Whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. And whatever you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Then Jesus warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Christ. Jesus speaks of his coming death.
From that time Jesus began to explain to His disciples what was going to happen to Him. He told them that He must go to Jerusalem. There He must suffer many things from the elders and chief priests and scribes. He must be killed and on the third day rise from the dead.
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "God forbid, Lord! This will never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are an obstacle to me. For you are not thinking about the things that are of God, but you are thinking about the things that are of men."
Then Jesus spoke to his disciples. He said, "If anyone wants to follow me, he must deny himself. He must take up his cross and follow me.
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it. But if he loses his life for me, he will find it.
What good would it do a person to gain the whole world and lose their own soul? Or what could a person possibly exchange for their own soul?
The Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father with His angels, and He will reward each one according to his works.
What I am about to tell you is true. Some of those who are standing here will not die before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.
Jesus' appearance was changed.
After six days, Jesus took Peter, James, and John the brother of James with him. He brought them up onto a high mountain. They were completely alone.
Before them, his appearance changed. His face shone like the sun. His clothes became white as light.
At that moment, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will put up three tents. One for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
While Peter was still speaking, a bright cloud came over them. And a voice out of the cloud said, "This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased. Listen to him!"
When the disciples heard this, they were terrified and fell to the ground with their faces down.
But Jesus came and touched them. And he said, "Arise, and do not be afraid."
When they looked up, they saw no one except Jesus.
They came down from the mountain. On their way down, Jesus told them what to do. He said, "Do not tell anyone what you have seen. Wait until the Son of Man has risen from the dead."
The disciples asked him, "Why do the teachers of the law say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered, "It is true. Elijah is supposed to come and make all things new again.
But I tell you, Elijah has come already. The people did not recognize him. They did to him everything they wanted to do. In the same way, they will make the Son of Man suffer.
Then the disciples understood that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy who had a demon.
As they came near to the crowd, a man approached Jesus. He knelt down before him on his knees.
He said, "Lord, have mercy on my son. He is trembling violently and suffering greatly. He often falls into the fire or into the water.
I brought him to your disciples. But they could not heal him."
Jesus said, "O ye people of Israel, which are ignorant and wicked! How long shall I be with you? How long shall I suffer you? Bring ye the child unto me."
Jesus commanded the demon to come out of the boy, and it came out. And he was healed at that very moment.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately. And they asked Him, "Why could we not drive out that demon?"
He answered, "Because you have so little faith. I tell you the truth, if you have faith as small as a mustard seed, you can say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there,' and it will move. Nothing will be impossible for you."
<.>
They met in Galilee. Then Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man will be delivered into the hands of men."
They will kill him. On the third day he will rise from the dead." Then the disciples were filled with grief. Jesus pays the temple tax.
Jesus and his disciples arrived in Capernaum. There, tax collectors came to Peter. They asked, "Does not your teacher pay the temple tax?"
"Yes, he does that," he answered. When Peter entered the house, Jesus spoke first. "What do you think, Simon?" he asked. "On whom do the kings of the earth impose taxes and fees? Do they impose them on their sons or on others?"
"From others," Peter replied. Jesus said to him, "Then the children ought not to pay."
But we do not want to anger them. So go to the lake and cast your line. Take the first fish you catch. Open its mouth. There you will find the exact coin you need. Take it and give it to them for my tax and your tax.
